# OXBOW local edge (DEV-023) -- the one origin a browser is allowed to learn.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# apps/web/src/lib/api/transport.ts sends *relative* /api paths from the page on purpose: a
# cross-origin POST cannot ride a simple request, Chrome blocks it on the preflight, no demo
# token is ever minted, and every later GET answers 401 -- which the envelope decoder then
# reports as a contract failure rather than as a wiring mistake. The `/api` rewrite in
# next.config.ts has been the thing that keeps that promise, and it is a poor place for it: it
# is applied after Node has loaded the app, it has no way to leave the run-progress SSE
# unbuffered per route, and the seam vanishes for anything that does not enter through
# `next start`. Caddy owns the seam here, at the edge, in front of both services.
#
# The rewrite stays in next.config.ts. It is what a bare `make web` with no edge in front still
# relies on, and removing it would break the path that exists today.
#
# NO TLS, DELIBERATELY
#
# Spec 12.9 makes `docker compose up` the reproducibility claim, and `tls internal` here would
# mean installing and trusting Caddy's local root CA on every machine that runs the demo before
# a browser would load the page at all. Host ports 80 and 443 are free on this box and are left
# unclaimed for the same reason: an edge that needs a privileged port is an edge nobody demos.

# Bound to the port rather than to a hostname, so localhost, 127.0.0.1 and the compose service
# name all reach the same site. Name a host here instead and Caddy matches on it, answering a
# request from any other Host with its no-site page -- which looks exactly like an app failure.
:{$OXBOW_EDGE_PORT:8080} {
	# The API owns its own prefix: apps/api/main.py mounts every data route under /api, so the
	# path is passed through unchanged. Stripping it would send /dashboard-matters to a router
	# that has never heard of that path.
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy api:8000 {
			# GET /api/runs/{id}/events/stream is `text/event-stream` (apps/api/events.py:288),
			# resumable with Last-Event-ID. -1 means flush every frame as it is produced instead
			# of letting the proxy accumulate one; with buffering on, the live run banner paints
			# all at once at the end, which is the failure the demo is specifically about.
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}

	# Everything else is the Next app, including its own /_next assets and RSC payloads.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy web:3000
	}
}
